243
00:10:09,430 --> 00:10:11,390
So, when I was practicing my spoken language skills,

244
00:10:11,390 --> 00:10:13,060
and they get nervous,

245
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:14,640
stumbling over their words, not knowing what to say.

246
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:16,380
So, my first thought would be to try and encourage them.

247
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:17,980
Don't worry, just relax a little.

248
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:18,860
We're all in the same boat.

249
00:10:18,860 --> 00:10:22,280
And I think that's just a way of showing kindness.

250
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:23,680
Right, so for the person on the receiving end,

251
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:25,400
and feel encouraged by them.

252
00:10:25,400 --> 00:10:27,200
I think that would make them feel more relaxed.

253
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:29,880
And the more relaxed they are, the easier it'll be for them to open up.

254
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:31,100
So, what's the solution?

255
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:32,840
You can do something while you're waiting.

256
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:33,680
and encourage the other person.

257
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:36,770
Looking at this interface now...

258
00:10:36,770 --> 00:10:40,380
Yeah, it's true, this one isn't all that impressive.

259
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:43,120
But the core idea behind this interaction design, I think that's really interesting.

260
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:46,480
And besides, this design is from almost 10 years ago, after all.

261
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:48,760
On the right side of this page, you can see the results.

262
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:53,150
The conversation completion rate increased from 51% to 65%.

263
00:10:53,150 --> 00:10:56,730
So, why did I choose the conversation completion rate as the key metric?

264
00:10:56,730 --> 00:10:58,710
Because the problem my design was meant to solve...

265
00:10:58,710 --> 00:11:01,700
is the anxiety users feel while waiting, which leads to them dropping out.

266
00:11:01,700 --> 00:11:04,640
So, the most direct piece of data that can prove this problem has been alleviated...

267
00:11:04,640 --> 00:11:06,100
is the conversation completion rate.

268
00:11:06,100 --> 00:11:07,420
And by 'dialogue completion' here,

269
00:11:07,420 --> 00:11:10,730
In other words, every conversation we have is based on a script.

270
00:11:10,730 --> 00:11:13,450
And the goal is for the two people to go back and forth and see the script through to the end.

271
00:11:13,450 --> 00:11:14,810
And that's what we call 'dialogue completion'.

272
00:11:14,810 --> 00:11:19,200
So, the increase in the completion rate shows that during the two-person conversation...

273
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:20,760
one person quits halfway through.

274
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:23,000
This kind of situation happened less often.

275
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,040
So, why did I still include the usage rate?

276
00:11:25,040 --> 00:11:29,220
So, first, I need to prove that people are actually using this new design.

277
00:11:29,220 --> 00:11:30,780
Otherwise, the increase in the conversation completion rate...

278
00:11:30,780 --> 00:11:33,520
might not have had much to do with my new design.

279
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:35,920
So, when you look at the usage rate and the completion rate together,

280
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:40,380
Only then can you prove that the design actually led to some improvement in performance.

281
00:11:40,660 --> 00:11:44,140
So, when a lot of UX designers are putting together their portfolios,

282
00:11:44,140 --> 00:11:45,040
They don't know what data to include.

283
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:48,730
